course: dictionary
lesson: 13344
title: Haggling and bargaining
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Haggling means arguing about the price of something to make it cheaper. You will hear it most at a market, where the seller and the buyer talk back and forth until they agree a price. «At the market, Amira and the seller spent ten minutes haggling over the rug.» The words «spent ten minutes» and «over the rug» tell you that they were talking about a price.
explain: Look at the places in the sentences. «In the supermarket, nobody haggles over the price on the label.» Supermarket prices are fixed, so there is no arguing. The clue is the word «nobody», which tells you haggling is not normal there.
explain: Maths problems can use haggling too. «After haggling, the price dropped from £20 to £15.» The price went down, so you can work out the discount by taking the new price away from the old one: £20 minus £15 is £5. Check your answer by swapping it back in: £15 plus £5 makes £20.
fact: Haggling means arguing about a price to make it lower.
fact: Haggling is normal at many markets, but not in supermarkets, where prices are on the label.
fact: «Haggling» is the -ing form of «haggle», so it can be a noun or part of a verb.
fact: A discount is the amount a price goes down by. «£20 to £15» is a discount of £5.
fact: A good check is to put your meaning into the sentence: «Amira and the seller argued about the price of the rug» still makes sense, so haggling is right.
exercise: «At the market, Amira and the seller spent ten minutes haggling over the rug.» What is haggling? | Walking slowly round a market | Giving a present to a friend | Counting coins in a shop | *Arguing about the price to make it cheaper
exercise: «After haggling, the price dropped from £20 to £15.» How much did the price come down by? | £20 | *£5 | £35 | £15
exercise: «In the supermarket, nobody haggles over the price on the label.» Where is haggling most likely to happen? | In a library | At the supermarket checkout | *At a busy market | On a bus
test: Which word means arguing about a price to get it lower? | Queuing | Measuring | *Haggling | Donating
test: «The seller said no at first, but after haggling for a while, she agreed to £8.» What does haggling mean here? | Shouting at the seller | *Arguing about the price until it comes down | Taking a photo of the rug | Handing over the money at once
exam: «Jamal wanted a cheaper bike, so he began haggling with the owner.» What is Jamal doing? | Buying the bike with no argument | *Trying to bargain the price down | Painting the bike | Riding away quickly
